window.COURSE_WEEKS = [{"num": 1, "title": "The 7-Step Framework", "subtitle": "The method every case study in this course uses", "tag": "Foundation", "readMin": 7, "html": "<h1>The 7-Step Case-Study Framework</h1>\n<p>Every system design round — startup or otherwise — rewards structure over cleverness. Interviewers aren't grading whether you know the \"right\" architecture; they're grading whether you can walk through a hard, ambiguous problem out loud, in order, without freezing. This framework is the order. Every case study in this course (Twitter, Instagram, Quora/Reddit, Google Search, and everything in the weeks that follow) is built by walking these same seven steps, so learn the steps cold before you learn any single system.</p>\n<h2>1. Defining Requirements</h2>\n<p>Split everything into <strong>functional requirements</strong> (what the system does — post a tweet, load a feed, run a search) and <strong>non-functional requirements</strong> (how well it does it — latency, availability, consistency, scale). Interviewers deliberately underspecify the problem; asking sharp clarifying questions before you draw anything is itself a scored signal. Silence followed by immediately sketching boxes reads as junior.</p>\n<p>Ask across four buckets — scale, behavior, consistency, constraints:\n1. How many daily active users, and what's the read:write ratio?\n2. What's the peak-to-average traffic ratio (any spiky events)?\n3. Does this need strong consistency anywhere, or is eventual consistency acceptable?\n4. What's the acceptable read latency (p99, not average)?\n5. Is this green-field or are we adding to existing infrastructure?\n6. What does the client look like — mobile, web, or both — and does that change payload constraints?\n7. Do we need to support media (images/video), or text-only?\n8. What's the data retention requirement — forever, or a rolling window?\n9. Is there a geographic/regulatory constraint (data residency, multi-region)?\n10. What's explicitly out of scope for this session (e.g., \"assume auth is already handled\")?</p>\n<p>Pick 5-6 relevant to the prompt; asking all ten burns time you need for designing.</p>\n<h2>2. Developing APIs</h2>\n<p>Sketch 4-6 endpoints, not the whole surface. Use plain REST naming: nouns, not verbs (<code>POST /tweets</code>, not <code>POST /createTweet</code>), plural resource names, and status codes you'd actually return. For each endpoint, state method, path, key request fields, and key response fields — one line each, not full JSON schemas.</p>\n<p>Default to REST for CRUD-shaped resources. Mention GraphQL when the client needs to compose data from many resources in one round trip and over-fetching is a real cost (e.g., a mobile feed screen pulling user + post + comment counts together) — say so explicitly rather than defaulting to it. Mention gRPC for service-to-service calls where you want strict typed contracts and binary-protocol latency over human-readable payloads. The signal you want to send: \"I know these exist and I know the trade-off that picks one,\" not \"I use GraphQL because it's modern.\"</p>\n<h2>3. Estimating Resources</h2>\n<p>Pure back-of-envelope math: QPS, storage, bandwidth, done live. Round aggressively — 86,400 seconds/day rounds to ~100,000 for mental math.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example — a tweet-like system, 200M DAU:</strong>\n- Writes: assume 2 tweets/user/day → 200M × 2 = 400M tweets/day. Write QPS = 400M ÷ 86,400 ≈ 4,600/sec average; at 3× peak, ~14,000/sec.\n- Reads: assume a 100:1 read:write ratio (feed-heavy system) → ~460,000 reads/sec average, ~1.4M/sec peak. This number alone is the argument for aggressive caching — you cannot hit a database at that rate.\n- Storage: average tweet ≈ 300 bytes (text + metadata). Text-only storage = 400M × 300B ≈ 120GB/day. If 10% of tweets carry media averaging 200KB, media adds 40M × 200KB ≈ 8TB/day. Total ≈ 8.1TB/day → ~3PB/year before replication; with 3× replication, ~9PB/year.\n- Bandwidth: 8.1TB/day ÷ 86,400s ≈ 94MB/sec average write bandwidth, ~280MB/sec peak.</p>\n<p>State assumptions out loud before the math (\"I'll assume 2 tweets per user per day\") — the interviewer grades the method, not whether your assumption matches the real company's numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference numbers worth memorizing</strong> (order of magnitude, not exact):\n- L1/L2 cache read: ~1-10ns · Main memory read: ~100ns\n- Round trip within same datacenter: ~0.5ms\n- SSD random read: ~100-150µs · Disk seek: ~10ms\n- Send 1MB over a 1Gbps network: ~10ms\n- Cross-continent round trip: ~150ms\n- Powers of 2: 2^10=1KB, 2^20=1MB, 2^30=1GB, 2^40=1TB, 2^50=1PB</p>\n<p>Say this out loud when it's relevant: memory is roughly 100,000× faster than a disk seek — that gap is <em>why</em> caching exists.</p>\n<h2>4. Modeling Data</h2>\n<p>Default question: <strong>SQL, NoSQL, or both?</strong> Choose SQL when you need multi-row transactions, joins across normalized entities, and strong consistency (payments, inventory, order state). Choose NoSQL when you need horizontal write scale, flexible/sparse schemas, or lookup-by-key access patterns (session data, activity feeds, high-volume event logs).</p>\n<p>Within NoSQL, pick by access pattern, not by hype:\n- <strong>Wide-column store</strong> (Cassandra, Bigtable/HBase) — massive write throughput, time-series/activity-log data, queried by partition key + sort key.\n- <strong>Document store</strong> (MongoDB, DynamoDB) — semi-structured records read/written as a whole object, moderate scale.\n- <strong>Graph store</strong> (Neo4j) — when the <em>relationships</em> are the query, not the entities (social graphs, recommendation traversal).</p>\n<p>For read-heavy systems, <strong>denormalize deliberately</strong>: duplicate data across tables/documents to avoid joins on the hot read path, and accept the write-side cost of keeping copies in sync (often via async jobs). Say explicitly: \"I'm denormalizing X into Y to avoid a join on the read path, and I'll reconcile them via an async update.\"</p>\n<h2>5. Creating the High-Level Design</h2>\n<p><div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>Client → Load Balancer → API/Service layer → (sync) Database + Cache, and → (async) Message Queue → Workers → Database, with CDN in front of the client for static/media assets</div></p>\n<p>A high-level design needs to show, left to right: client → load balancer → API/service layer → data layer (with cache alongside it) → async workers/queue for anything that doesn't need to block the response → CDN for anything static or geographically distributed. Narrate it as a <strong>request's journey</strong>, not a list of boxes: \"A client hits the load balancer, which routes to a stateless API server; that server checks cache first, falls back to the database on a miss, and pushes anything non-urgent — like a notification — onto a queue for a worker to handle asynchronously.\" Naming the <em>flow</em> is what separates this step from step 6 — here you stay shallow on every box.</p>\n<h2>6. Detailing Components</h2>\n<p>Don't go deep everywhere — pick the 1-2 components where the actual hard problem lives, and say why you're picking them before you dive in (\"the interesting part isn't the API layer, it's how we generate the feed, so let's go deep there\"). Structure each deep-dive identically: state the specific sub-problem, state 2 candidate approaches, state the trade-off, state your pick and why it fits the requirements from step 1. This is where most of your interview score actually comes from — a shallow pass over everything reads worse than real depth on the two things that matter.</p>\n<h2>7. Addressing Bottlenecks and Scaling</h2>\n<p>A one-line catalog you should be able to deploy on demand:\n- <strong>Cache-aside</strong> — app checks cache first, loads from DB and populates cache on a miss.\n- <strong>Write-through cache</strong> — writes go to cache and DB together, keeping cache always fresh.\n- <strong>Write-back cache</strong> — writes hit cache first and flush to DB asynchronously, trading durability risk for write speed.\n- <strong>Horizontal sharding</strong> — split data across nodes by a shard key to scale writes beyond one machine.\n- <strong>Consistent hashing</strong> — a hashing scheme that minimizes data movement when nodes are added/removed from a sharded/cached cluster.\n- <strong>Read replicas</strong> — copies of the primary DB that serve reads, offloading the write-primary.\n- <strong>CDN</strong> — edge caching for static/media content close to the user.\n- <strong>Message queues</strong> — decouple producers from consumers so slow downstream work doesn't block the request.\n- <strong>Rate limiting</strong> — cap requests per client/key to protect the system from abuse or overload.\n- <strong>Load balancing algorithms</strong> — round robin, least connections, or consistent-hash-based routing to distribute traffic.\n- <strong>Connection pooling</strong> — reuse DB connections instead of opening one per request.\n- <strong>Eventual consistency</strong> — accepting a delay before all replicas agree, in exchange for availability/throughput.\n- <strong>Circuit breakers</strong> — stop calling a failing downstream dependency for a cooldown period instead of retrying into it.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong> functional vs. non-functional requirements, back-of-envelope estimation, p99 latency, read:write ratio, denormalization, wide-column store, cache-aside, write-through, write-back, horizontal sharding, consistent hashing, read replica, message queue, rate limiting, circuit breaker.</p>"}, {"num": 2, "title": "Social Feed & Graph", "subtitle": "Twitter (flagship) · Instagram · Quora / Reddit", "tag": "Family A", "readMin": 7, "html": "<h1>Family A — Social Feed &amp; Graph Systems</h1>\n<p>Feed generation, social-graph modeling, and ranking are among the most reused patterns in startup interviews, because almost any product with users following or subscribing to other users or content eventually needs a personalized feed — not just literal social networks. The core hard problem is always the same: turning one write into potentially millions of reads, fast, without recomputing everything on every page load. Master this family once and you can adapt it to a notifications feed, an activity log, a marketplace \"following\" feed, or a B2B dashboard update stream. Twitter is the flagship because it has the sharpest version of every sub-problem — feed fan-out, the celebrity/hot-key problem, and trending computation — and the two siblings below are read as deltas against it.</p>\n<h2>Twitter (Microblogging)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining Requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional: post a tweet (text + optional media), follow/unfollow, view home timeline (posts from people you follow), view a user's own tweets, search/hashtags. Non-functional: read-heavy (users check timeline far more than they post), timeline reads must be low-latency (sub-second), eventual consistency is fine (a tweet appearing a few seconds late in a follower's feed is acceptable), availability over strict consistency. Clarifying questions: chronological or ranked timeline? Do high-follower accounts need special handling? Is media required in v1? What's the retention requirement?</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>POST /tweets</code> — body: user_id, text, media_url? → returns tweet_id, created_at</li>\n<li><code>GET /timeline/home?cursor=</code> — paginated tweet list for the current user's feed</li>\n<li><code>POST /follows</code> — body: follower_id, followee_id</li>\n<li><code>GET /tweets/{id}</code> — single tweet detail</li>\n<li><code>GET /trends</code> — current trending hashtags</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plain REST is sufficient externally; gRPC would fit the internal fan-out-service-to-cache calls if asked about internal architecture.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating Resources</h3>\n<p>Assume 250M DAU, avg 2 tweets/user/day → 500M tweets/day, write QPS ≈ 5,800/sec average (~17,000 peak at 3×). Average follower count: assume 200 (most accounts are small; a handful are huge — this asymmetry is exactly the celebrity problem below). Fan-out writes/day (if fanning out every tweet to every follower's timeline) = 500M tweets × 200 followers ≈ 100 billion timeline-insert operations/day → ~1.16M fan-out writes/sec average. That number alone tells you pure fan-out-on-write doesn't survive a celebrity: one tweet from a 100M-follower account is 100M individual writes fired at once.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling Data</h3>\n<p>Core entities: User, Tweet, Follow (edge), Timeline (materialized per-user list of tweet_ids). The social graph is naturally graph-shaped, but at this scale it's usually modeled as two wide-column-style tables (followers-of, following-of) rather than a literal graph DB, because the query pattern is \"give me the list,\" not deep traversal. Tweet content lives in a document/wide-column store keyed by tweet_id (write-once, read-many). The timeline itself is a denormalized, precomputed list per user (cache/wide-column), traded off against the fan-out cost above.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the High-Level Design</h3>\n<p><div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>Client → LB → API layer → (write path) Fan-out Service → per-user Timeline Cache, with Tweet Store written once; (read path) API layer → Timeline Cache → hydrate from Tweet Cache</div></p>\n<p>A post hits the API layer, is written once to the tweet store, then handed to an async fan-out service that pushes the tweet_id into the precomputed timeline cache of each follower. A timeline read never touches fan-out logic — it just reads the follower's precomputed list from cache and hydrates the actual content from a separate tweet cache.</p>\n<h3>6. Detailing Components</h3>\n<p>The one component worth a real deep-dive is feed generation — specifically fan-out. Everything else (auth, tweet CRUD) is standard CRUD and shouldn't eat interview time.</p>\n<p><strong>Fan-out-on-write (push):</strong> on every tweet, immediately push the tweet_id into every follower's precomputed timeline. Reads become a cache lookup — very fast. Breaks down for celebrities: one popular tweet becomes millions of writes on the critical path.</p>\n<p><strong>Fan-out-on-read (pull):</strong> precompute nothing; when a user opens their timeline, fetch recent tweets from everyone they follow and merge on the fly. Cheap on write, expensive on read, and cost grows with how many accounts you follow.</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid (what real systems do):</strong> fan-out-on-write for regular accounts; for accounts above a follower threshold, skip the push and merge their tweets in at read-time instead. This caps worst-case write amplification while keeping the common case fast.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing Bottlenecks and Scaling</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hot key / celebrity problem</strong> — solved by the hybrid fan-out above, not by \"just add more cache nodes.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Timeline cache</strong> — a per-user sorted structure (e.g., a sorted set keyed by user_id, capped to ~800 recent tweet_ids); tweet content lives in a separate cache keyed by tweet_id to avoid duplicating full tweet bodies across every follower's entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Trending/hashtags</strong> — computed off the write path: a stream-processing layer counts hashtag occurrences in sliding time windows (e.g., last 10 minutes) and maintains a periodically refreshed top-K list, not recalculated per request.</li>\n<li><strong>Sharding</strong> — tweet store sharded by tweet_id (or user_id for a user's own tweets); follow-graph sharded by user_id with edges duplicated for both \"who follows me\" and \"who do I follow\" lookups.</li>\n<li><strong>Async queue</strong> — fan-out itself runs through a queue/worker pool so a posting spike doesn't block the write API.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong> fan-out on write, fan-out on read, hybrid fan-out, hot key / celebrity problem, denormalized timeline cache, precomputed feed, social graph, edge (follower/following), write amplification, sliding window aggregation, top-K trending, eventual consistency.</p>\n<h2>Instagram — What's Different</h2>\n<p>Instagram's core feed problem is the same fan-out/celebrity mechanic as Twitter's step 6 — the genuine differences are in steps 3 and 4, and in which component earns the step-6 deep-dive.</p>\n<p><strong>Media storage at scale.</strong> Where Twitter's step 4 stores mostly small text records, Instagram's dominant storage cost is media, not text. Assume 500M DAU posting ~1 photo/user/day at ~1MB average → ~500TB/day of raw media before compression — two to three orders of magnitude more than Twitter's text-heavy estimate. This changes step 4's answer: metadata (captions, likes, user_id) still lives in a fast key-value/document store, but the media blobs go to object storage, never the primary database.</p>\n<p><strong>CDN and image-processing pipeline.</strong> This becomes the step-6 deep-dive instead of fan-out: an upload triggers an async pipeline that generates multiple resolutions/thumbnails, compresses, and pushes results to a CDN. The read path never serves the original upload — it serves the CDN-cached, pre-resized variant closest to the requesting client. Same \"push work off the critical path via a queue\" idea as Twitter's step 7, applied to transcoding instead of fan-out.</p>\n<p><strong>Feed ranking differences.</strong> Twitter's flagship design is closer to reverse-chronological with hybrid fan-out; Instagram's feed is ranked by an ML-scored relevance model (recency, relationship strength, past engagement) computed at read time over a candidate set assembled via the same fan-out mechanics from step 6. In an interview, it's enough to say: \"fan-out gets you the <em>candidate</em> posts; a separate ranking step re-orders them before serving\" — you're not expected to design the ranking model itself.</p>\n<h2>Quora / Reddit — What's Different</h2>\n<p>Quora and Reddit share Twitter's step-5 high-level shape (API → fan-out/feed → cache) but diverge in step 4 (data modeling) and step 6 (the deep-dive component).</p>\n<p><strong>Comment/reply tree modeling.</strong> Unlike a tweet (flat, standalone), a Reddit/Quora post has a nested comment tree that can go dozens of levels deep. Modeling this well is the step-4 delta: store each comment with a parent_id plus a materialized path (e.g., <code>1.4.2</code>) so a whole subtree can be fetched without recursive queries at read time. An adjacency-list-only model forces N recursive queries to render a thread — the fix is precomputing the path at write time, the same denormalization principle as Twitter's timeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranking as the step-6 deep-dive instead of fan-out.</strong> Answer/comment ranking (\"hot\"/\"best\" algorithms, answer-quality ranking) combines vote counts with time-decay so old popular content doesn't permanently bury new good content — conceptually <code>votes / (age_in_hours + 2)^gravity</code>. This, not fan-out, is the component worth narrating deeply, because the hard problem shifted from \"get this to followers fast\" to \"order this correctly.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Content moderation.</strong> Both platforms need a moderation layer (automated flagging + human/community review queues) between write and publish, which Twitter's flagship doesn't emphasize — worth mentioning as an async, queue-based pipeline (same async pattern as step 7).</p>\n<p><strong>Community-based partitioning.</strong> Data is naturally partitioned by subreddit/topic rather than by user, shifting the sharding key from Twitter's user_id-based approach to a community_id-based one — a hot community becomes the new \"celebrity problem.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong> fan-out on write, fan-out on read, hybrid fan-out, hot key / celebrity problem, denormalized timeline cache, precomputed feed, social graph, edge (follower/following), write amplification, sliding window aggregation, top-K trending, eventual consistency.</p>"}, {"num": 3, "title": "Real-Time Messaging", "subtitle": "WhatsApp (flagship) · Slack/Discord · Zoom/Meet · Gmail", "tag": "Family B", "readMin": 11, "html": "<h1>Family B — Real-Time Messaging &amp; Communication Systems</h1>\n<p>Startups building anything social, collaborative, or support-oriented almost always end up needing some form of real-time or near-real-time communication — chat, notifications, live support, team collaboration — which is why this pattern family shows up constantly in startup system design rounds. Interviewers use it because it forces you to reason about problems that don't exist in typical CRUD systems: delivering something exactly when the recipient may or may not be reachable, guaranteeing a message isn't lost or duplicated, and keeping millions of idle connections open cheaply. If you can walk through WhatsApp cleanly, you can adapt the same reasoning to a support-chat widget, a notification service, or a collaborative editor — which is exactly what a ₹12-15 LPA startup interview is testing: can you generalize a pattern, not recite a memorized diagram.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: WhatsApp</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining Requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional requirements: users send and receive text and media messages one-to-one and in groups; senders see delivery status (sent → delivered → read); messages reach recipients who are currently offline as soon as they reconnect; content is end-to-end encrypted so no intermediary, including WhatsApp's own servers, can read it. Explicitly out of scope for this case study (state this out loud in an interview — it shows scoping discipline): voice/video calling, Status/Stories, and payments are separate systems with different transport requirements.</p>\n<p>Non-functional requirements: low latency for online delivery (sub-200ms feels instant); high availability (a messaging app going down is a trust-breaking outage); durability — a message must never silently vanish; scale to hundreds of millions of daily active users; and a specific consistency posture — per-conversation message ordering must be strict, but global things like \"last seen\" or read receipts can be eventually consistent.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p>Because delivery needs to be push-based, not poll-based, the primary interface is a persistent connection (WebSocket or long-lived TCP) rather than plain REST, but you still define a clear contract over it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>connect(authToken)</code> — establishes and authenticates the persistent connection.</li>\n<li><code>sendMessage(conversationId, clientMessageId, ciphertext, mediaRef?)</code> → server responds with <code>ack(serverMessageId, serverTimestamp)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>deliveryAck(messageId)</code> / <code>readAck(messageId)</code> — client-to-server signals that update status.</li>\n<li><code>fetchMissedMessages(sinceMessageId)</code> — called on reconnect to pull anything sent while offline.</li>\n<li><code>getMediaUploadUrl()</code> → returns a presigned URL for direct blob upload.</li>\n<li><code>presence.subscribe(userIds)</code> — opt-in online/last-seen updates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The detail worth saying out loud in an interview: <code>clientMessageId</code> is an idempotency key. Mobile networks retry silently; without a client-generated ID that the server can deduplicate on, a flaky connection produces duplicate messages.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating Resources</h3>\n<p>State your assumptions before you calculate — that's the skill being graded, not the final digit. Assume 500M DAU and an average of 40 messages sent per user per day.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total messages/day = 500M × 40 = <strong>20 billion messages/day</strong>.</li>\n<li>Average throughput = 20B ÷ 86,400s ≈ <strong>230K messages/sec</strong>; at a 3x evening peak multiplier, ≈ <strong>700K messages/sec at peak</strong>.</li>\n<li>Average text payload (ciphertext + metadata) ≈ 100 bytes → 20B × 100B = <strong>2TB/day</strong> of message traffic (this is flow, not standing storage — delivered messages are purged from servers, which matters for the E2E-encryption story).</li>\n<li>Assume 10% of messages carry media, averaging 250KB each: 2B media messages/day × 250KB = <strong>500TB/day</strong> of media traffic — two to three orders of magnitude bigger than text, which is exactly why media can't live in the same store as message metadata.</li>\n<li>Assume 30% of DAU is concurrently connected at peak = <strong>150M concurrent persistent connections</strong> to size your connection-server fleet against.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Modeling Data</h3>\n<p>Core entities: <code>Users</code>, <code>Conversations</code> (1:1 and group), <code>Messages</code>, <code>GroupMembership</code>. A message row holds <code>message_id</code>, <code>conversation_id</code>, <code>sender_id</code>, <code>client_message_id</code> (for dedup), <code>ciphertext</code>, <code>media_ref</code> (nullable), <code>timestamp</code>, and <code>status</code>. Messages are sharded by <code>conversation_id</code> so a whole conversation's ordering lives on one partition.</p>\n<p>Critically, there's a separate, short-lived structure: a per-recipient <strong>offline mailbox/queue</strong>, holding only messages not yet delivery-acked. Once delivered, the entry is purged — WhatsApp's servers are not a permanent message archive; that's what makes the \"server can't read your history\" claim credible.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the High-Level Design</h3>\n<p>Client ↔ <strong>Connection Servers</strong> (hold the persistent socket, handle auth and presence) ↔ <strong>Message Service</strong> (dedup, ordering, routing logic) ↔ <strong>Recipient Mailbox Store</strong> (durable offline queue) + <strong>Media Blob Store + CDN</strong> ↔ <strong>Push Notification Service</strong> (APNs/FCM, to wake a fully backgrounded app) ↔ <strong>Presence Service</strong>. Encryption itself is not a server component at all — it happens entirely client-side; the server only ever relays ciphertext.</p>\n<p><div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>boxes for Client, Connection Server, Message Service, Mailbox Store, Media Blob Storage + CDN, Push Notification Service, and Presence Service, with arrows showing the online-delivery path and a separate dashed path for the offline/push-wake flow.</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing Components</h3>\n<p><strong>Connection servers</strong> hold per-user session state and are intentionally cheap-per-connection (this is why WhatsApp's real infrastructure famously used Erlang — lightweight processes for millions of mostly-idle sockets). A <strong>presence/routing layer</strong> (Redis-backed) maps <code>user_id → which connection server they're attached to</code>, since a message has to be routed across servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Online delivery path</strong>: sender's connection server → message service → routing lookup finds recipient's connection server → push to that server → deliver to socket → delivery ack flows back the same path → sender's UI updates the tick marks.</p>\n<p><strong>Offline path</strong>: message service can't find the recipient online, so it writes to their durable mailbox and fires a push notification. On reconnect, the client calls <code>fetchMissedMessages</code> from its last-seen ID. This store-and-forward behavior is the core mechanism that makes offline users a non-issue rather than a failure case.</p>\n<p><strong>End-to-end encryption</strong>: built on the Signal protocol. Each device publishes an identity key and a batch of one-time prekeys; a sender fetches the recipient's prekey bundle once and derives a shared session key (X3DH), after which a Double Ratchet algorithm rotates keys per message so compromising one message's key doesn't expose the rest of the conversation. Groups use a shared sender key distributed once per membership change rather than pairwise encryption per message. The server never holds a decryption key — it is structurally incapable of reading content, not just policy-restricted from it.</p>\n<p><strong>Media</strong>: the client uploads the file to blob storage first, encrypts it client-side, and gets back a reference; the actual message sent over the connection carries only that reference plus the decryption key — \"upload once, share a reference,\" never the bytes themselves through the message pipe.</p>\n<p><strong>Ordering and idempotency</strong>: the server assigns a monotonic per-conversation sequence (or server timestamp) for display order; delivery from server to client is <strong>at-least-once</strong> (a missed ack triggers redelivery), so the client deduplicates on <code>message_id</code>, not the server.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing Bottlenecks and Scaling</h3>\n<p>Shard connection servers and the mailbox store by <code>user_id</code> hash so no single node owns a disproportionate share of sockets or queues. Cache the presence/routing table in-memory (Redis) since it's read on every single message route. Large groups create write-amplification (one message becomes N fan-out writes) — past a threshold, shift from fan-out-on-write toward a hybrid or fan-out-on-read model to avoid overwhelming write throughput for group sizes in the thousands. Media bandwidth and storage cost dominate at scale, so lean on CDN edge caching for frequently re-shared media and tiered/cold storage for the rest. Finally, replicate mailbox and routing state across data centers for both durability and geo-latency, routing each user to their nearest connection server.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Deltas</h2>\n<h3>Slack / Discord</h3>\n<p>The biggest structural change from WhatsApp is that <strong>channels and workspaces become first-class entities</strong>, not just conversations — a channel has its own membership, permissions, and an effectively permanent, searchable history, unlike WhatsApp's ephemeral store-and-forward model where the server doesn't retain delivered messages. That single fact reshapes the data model and the whole system: you now need a durable message archive per channel plus a <strong>full-text search index</strong> (something like Elasticsearch) over potentially billions of historical messages, because \"search my workspace's history\" is a core feature, not an edge case.</p>\n<p>Presence also stops working the WhatsApp way. WhatsApp tracks precise per-contact online status and per-message delivery/read receipts because conversations are small. In a 10,000-member Discord server or Slack workspace, broadcasting live presence and per-member read receipts to everyone doesn't scale and isn't even useful — so these platforms use coarser online/idle/offline heartbeats and a cursor-style \"last read message ID\" per user per channel instead of individual receipts per recipient.</p>\n<p>Fan-out direction flips too: WhatsApp pushes a message to every recipient's mailbox immediately (fan-out-on-write) because group sizes are small and real-time delivery matters. In a channel with thousands of members, that write amplification is too costly relative to the benefit, so channel messages lean toward a <strong>pull/fan-out-on-read</strong> model — closer to a timeline the client fetches — with real-time push layered on top only for currently-open channels.</p>\n<p>Finally, the permission model is far richer than WhatsApp's binary \"you're a group member or you're not\": roles (admin, moderator, member, guest) gate read/write/manage actions per channel, which means every message write and read needs an authorization check against a role/ACL table that has no WhatsApp equivalent at all.</p>\n<h3>Zoom / Google Meet</h3>\n<p>This is a genuinely different transport problem, not a variant of the same one. Messaging optimizes for correctness and durability over latency; live video/audio optimizes for latency and jitter over correctness — dropping a video frame is fine, buffering for 300ms is not. That's why video calls run over <strong>WebRTC/UDP-based RTP</strong> instead of a reliable TCP/WebSocket channel: retransmitting a lost packet late is worse than just losing it.</p>\n<p>For multi-party calls, the architectural fork is <strong>SFU vs MCU</strong>. An MCU (Multipoint Control Unit) decodes every participant's stream, mixes them server-side into one composite video, and sends each participant a single pre-mixed stream — cheap on client bandwidth and decoding, very expensive in server CPU. An SFU (Selective Forwarding Unit) does none of that: it receives each participant's stream once and simply forwards (routes) copies to the other participants, who decode multiple incoming streams themselves. SFUs are the modern default (Zoom, Meet) because client hardware easily handles decoding several streams and it's far cheaper to scale a router than a mixer.</p>\n<p>Adaptive bitrate here works differently from streaming video too: with <strong>simulcast</strong>, each sending client encodes and uploads multiple quality layers simultaneously, and the SFU picks which layer to forward to each receiver based on that receiver's measured bandwidth — the adaptation decision happens per-receiver at the SFU, not by the sender guessing one bitrate for everyone.</p>\n<p>Resource estimation is dominated by bandwidth, not storage or database throughput: an SFU forwarding N participants to each other costs roughly O(N) egress bandwidth per participant, so Zoom's scaling bottleneck is routing and network capacity, never a message log or a mailbox store — there usually isn't persistent storage at all unless a call is explicitly recorded.</p>\n<h3>Gmail</h3>\n<p>Gmail has no real-time expectation whatsoever — asynchronous store-and-forward isn't a fallback path like WhatsApp's offline handling, it's the entire model. Transfer between mail servers uses SMTP, a fire-and-forget protocol with its own retry queues; clients then pull mailbox state via IMAP-style access rather than holding a persistent push connection — the whole system is closer to a pull-based API than to WhatsApp's connection-server architecture. Delivery/read receipts, where they exist at all, are an optional courtesy, not a protocol guarantee.</p>\n<p>The data model's key difference is <strong>labels and threads instead of channels or conversation IDs</strong>: a message belongs to a thread and can carry multiple labels in a many-to-many relationship, rather than WhatsApp's one-message-belongs-to-one-conversation structure. Storage is dominated by mailbox size per user — years of retained messages and attachments — rather than a high-throughput low-latency log, which makes this fundamentally a <strong>search-and-storage problem</strong>: every mailbox needs its own efficient full-text and metadata index at a scale of billions of mailboxes.</p>\n<p>A pipeline with zero WhatsApp analog sits in front of delivery entirely: <strong>spam filtering</strong>. Sender reputation, content signals, and engagement signals feed a classifier before a message is even placed in an inbox — this is a whole subsystem WhatsApp's design doesn't need in the same way. Resource estimation shifts accordingly: instead of messages/sec and concurrent sockets, you'd estimate total stored bytes (average mailbox size × number of users) and index size, because durability and search-at-rest — not connection fan-out — dominate the cost.</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud</h2>\n<p><strong>At-least-once delivery · Idempotency key · Store-and-forward · Presence service · Delivery/read receipts · Fan-out-on-write vs fan-out-on-read · Per-conversation message ordering (sequence numbers) · End-to-end encryption (Signal protocol / Double Ratchet) · SFU vs MCU · WebRTC · Adaptive bitrate via simulcast · Connection server · Persistent connection (WebSocket/long-lived TCP) · Full-text search index (for history/search-heavy variants)</strong></p>"}, {"num": 4, "title": "Media Streaming & Recs", "subtitle": "YouTube (flagship) · Netflix · Spotify", "tag": "Family C", "readMin": 10, "html": "<h1>Family C — Media Streaming &amp; Recommendation Systems</h1>\n<p>Nearly every consumer startup with content — video, audio, articles, even product catalogs — eventually needs to answer two questions: how do you store and serve large media efficiently at scale, and how do you decide what to show each user next. That's exactly what this pattern family tests, and it's a favorite in startup interviews because it combines two distinct hard problems — a storage/delivery pipeline and a ranking/recommendation system — that most candidates only know how to talk about separately. Nailing YouTube as a case study proves you can reason about both an asynchronous processing pipeline and a two-stage ranking system in the same design, which is precisely the muscle a startup building any kind of content or discovery product needs from a senior engineer.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: YouTube</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining Requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional requirements: users upload video, which gets processed into multiple playable qualities; viewers stream video that adapts to their bandwidth; the platform surfaces search results and personalized recommendations; view counts and watch-time analytics are tracked. State scope explicitly: comments, monetization/ads, and live streaming are separate systems for this case study.</p>\n<p>Non-functional requirements: this system is read-heavy to an extreme degree — views vastly outnumber uploads — so availability and latency for playback matter far more than for the upload path; durability of stored video is non-negotiable (you can't lose an uploaded file); playback should start within 1-2 seconds; view counts and recommendation freshness can be eventually consistent; the system must serve a global audience, which pushes toward geographic distribution from day one.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>POST /videos/upload</code> → returns a resumable/chunked upload URL directly to blob storage (never through the application server).</li>\n<li><code>POST /videos/{id}/complete</code> → marks upload done and enqueues transcoding.</li>\n<li><code>GET /videos/{id}/manifest</code> → returns an adaptive streaming manifest (HLS <code>.m3u8</code> or DASH <code>.mpd</code>) listing available renditions and segment URLs.</li>\n<li><code>GET</code> on the actual video segments is served by the <strong>CDN</strong>, not the API layer, at all — this separation is deliberate and important to say out loud.</li>\n<li><code>POST /videos/{id}/view</code> → fires an event asynchronously into an analytics pipeline; it does not synchronously increment a counter.</li>\n<li><code>GET /recommendations?userId=</code> and <code>GET /search?q=</code> → each backed by its own dedicated service, not the video metadata database.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Estimating Resources</h3>\n<p>State assumptions, then compute. Assume 200M DAU, 40 minutes average watch time per user per day, and 500 hours of new video uploaded per minute.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total watch time/day = 200M × 40 min = 8B minutes ≈ <strong>133M hours watched/day</strong>. At a blended adaptive-bitrate average of ~2Mbps (≈900MB/hour), that's 133M × 900MB ≈ <strong>120 petabytes/day of egress bandwidth</strong> — roughly <strong>11 terabits/sec sustained average</strong>. No single origin can push that; this number alone is the justification for a CDN, not an optimization on top of the design.</li>\n<li>Uploads: 500 hours/min × 1,440 min/day = <strong>720,000 hours of raw video uploaded per day</strong>. At a source bitrate of ~8Mbps (≈3.6GB/hour), that's ≈<strong>2.6 petabytes/day of raw ingest</strong>, growing to roughly <strong>4-5 petabytes/day once transcoded into multiple renditions</strong>.</li>\n<li>View-start throughput: assume 10 plays/user/day → 2B plays/day ÷ 86,400s ≈ <strong>23K plays/sec average</strong>, <strong>~70K/sec at peak</strong> (3x multiplier) — this is the number that sizes your metadata-lookup and manifest-serving path.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Modeling Data</h3>\n<p><code>Videos</code>: <code>video_id</code>, <code>uploader_id</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>status</code> (processing/ready/failed), <code>duration</code>, a list of <code>rendition</code> pointers (resolution → blob storage key), thumbnail references. As with WhatsApp's media, <strong>the database stores only references — never the video bytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>View counts and watch-time are deliberately <em>not</em> columns updated synchronously on the video row — a viral video would create massive write contention on one row (a hot key). Instead, view events stream into a log and are aggregated asynchronously into an approximate, frequently-refreshed counter, with periodic exact reconciliation.</p>\n<p>Recommendation data lives in its own store entirely: a large event log of user-video interactions (watch, skip, like, watch-time-completed), feeding offline feature pipelines and embeddings — this is intentionally decoupled from the video metadata database because it's written and read in a completely different pattern (batch-heavy, not transactional).</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the High-Level Design</h3>\n<p>Client → <strong>Upload Service</strong> → raw <strong>Blob Storage</strong> → <strong>Transcoding Pipeline</strong> (async job queue) → writes renditions to Blob Storage, fronted by a <strong>CDN</strong> → <strong>Video Metadata Service</strong> flips status to ready. Playback: Client → CDN edge (majority of requests resolve here) → origin Blob Storage only on a cache miss. Running alongside, as separate services: <strong>Search</strong>, <strong>Recommendation</strong>, and an <strong>Analytics/view-count stream pipeline</strong>.</p>\n<p><div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>a pipeline flow — Upload → Raw Storage → Transcoding Queue → Renditions in Blob Storage + CDN → Playback via CDN edge — with Metadata DB, Search Index, Recommendation Service, and Analytics Stream drawn as separate side-services all reading from or writing to the pipeline, not inline with it.</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing Components</h3>\n<p><strong>Transcoding pipeline</strong>: deliberately asynchronous, because encoding is CPU-heavy and can take minutes — doing it synchronously on upload would block the uploader for no benefit. A job queue picks up the raw file and runs parallel jobs per target resolution and bitrate (144p through 4K, sometimes several bitrates per resolution), writing each rendition independently. This decoupling also means you can prioritize: produce a fast 480p rendition first so the video is watchable sooner, then backfill higher resolutions.</p>\n<p><strong>Storage tiering</strong>: recently uploaded and currently popular videos are cached broadly at CDN edge nodes; long-tail, rarely-watched videos stay in cheaper origin storage and are fetched on the rare occasion someone requests them — accepting higher latency for content nobody expects to be fast.</p>\n<p><strong>Adaptive bitrate streaming</strong>: each rendition is chunked into short segments (2-10 seconds). The client player continuously measures its own bandwidth and buffer health and requests the next segment at whichever rendition currently fits — that's what lets a stream survive a bandwidth drop without stalling or restarting, and it's exactly what the HLS/DASH manifest exists to describe.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation system, two stages</strong>: <strong>candidate generation</strong> cheaply narrows the entire catalog down to a few hundred or thousand plausible videos per user — using collaborative filtering (patterns across similar users), content-based embedding similarity, or simple signals like subscriptions and trending — because scoring the full catalog per request is computationally infeasible. A heavier <strong>ranking</strong> model then scores only that shortlist using richer features (predicted watch time, freshness, diversity) to produce the final ordered list. These are genuinely separate services because they're updated on different cadences — candidate generation can run more often and more cheaply than the full ranking model. New videos with no interaction history yet lean on content-based signals since collaborative filtering has nothing to work with — this is the <strong>cold-start problem</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Analytics pipeline</strong>: view-start and watch-time events stream in, are aggregated near-real-time for a fast approximate count, and reconciled exactly on a slower cadence — never blocking the playback path on writing a count.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing Bottlenecks and Scaling</h3>\n<p>The read/write ratio is extreme, so nearly every optimization favors the read path: the CDN absorbing the overwhelming majority of playback bandwidth is what makes origin storage cost tractable at all. A video going unexpectedly viral creates a hot-spot (\"thundering herd\") against a cache that wasn't pre-warmed for it — mitigate with dynamic edge replication triggered by request-rate spikes. Upload spikes create transcoding-queue backpressure — handle with a horizontally scalable worker pool, optionally prioritized by expected popularity. Recommendation cost is dominated by ranking-model inference, not candidate generation, so cache ranked results per user for short windows rather than recomputing on every page load. Accept eventual consistency for view counts outright — never let a counter write block or slow playback. Globally, route users to the nearest CDN edge via anycast/geo-DNS and replicate metadata across regions to keep reads low-latency everywhere.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Deltas</h2>\n<h3>Netflix</h3>\n<p>The foundational difference is the catalog itself: Netflix's content is <strong>licensed and curated</strong>, not user-generated, so there's no upload/transcoding-from-the-public pipeline at all, and — critically — the catalog is comparatively small and fully known in advance (thousands of titles, not an unbounded long tail). That single fact flips the caching problem on its head: instead of YouTube's reactive approach (cache what's suddenly popular), Netflix can proactively pre-position nearly its <strong>entire catalog</strong> at the edge ahead of time. This is why Netflix built Open Connect — physical caching appliances placed inside ISP data centers — achieving near-100% edge cache hit rates that YouTube's long tail makes structurally impossible.</p>\n<p>Personalization goes further than YouTube's single ranked feed: Netflix pre-computes many distinct ranked <strong>rows</strong> per user (Continue Watching, Because You Watched X, Trending in Your Region), each generated by a different candidate-generation strategy, then assembles them into one homepage. That's more ranking computations per user than YouTube's single feed — but run over a vastly smaller candidate pool, which is exactly why it's tractable to mostly pre-compute these offline and refresh periodically, rather than ranking live per request the way YouTube's single feed often does.</p>\n<p>Licensing introduces a dimension with no YouTube equivalent: content availability is <strong>region-locked by contract</strong>, so the metadata layer must carry per-region rights and expiry dates, and both search and recommendation candidates must be filtered by \"is this licensed in the viewer's region right now\" before any ranking happens at all. Offline downloads are a first-class, license-bounded, DRM-wrapped feature here — requiring a rights/key-management layer that a base YouTube design simply doesn't need.</p>\n<p>In short: trade YouTube's \"infinite long-tail UGC, reactive edge caching, single ranked feed\" for \"small licensed catalog, proactive full-catalog edge distribution, many pre-computed personalized rows, and region-aware rights management.\"</p>\n<h3>Spotify</h3>\n<p>Audio instead of video shrinks the hardest part of the YouTube problem by two to three orders of magnitude: a four-minute track at a good codec is a few megabytes, versus gigabytes for a video, so the storage/transcoding/bandwidth machinery that dominates YouTube's design barely matters here — a handful of bitrate renditions per track is cheap to store and serve.</p>\n<p>Recommendation shifts from video-level to <strong>track-level and playlist-level</strong>, and gets denser: users move through dozens of short tracks per session rather than one longer video, generating faster, more granular interaction signals. Spotify layers playlist modeling on top of per-track recommendation — algorithmically generated playlists (Discover Weekly-style) are their own data entity, produced by a batch job on a weekly cadence, distinct from the near-real-time next-track recommendation used during active listening. This pushes the data model to add a first-class <strong>playlist</strong> entity — an ordered collection of track references — shared structurally between user-created and algorithm-generated playlists.</p>\n<p><strong>Gapless playback</strong> is a streaming-quality requirement with no YouTube analog: consecutive tracks, especially within an album or a mixed set, must transition with zero audible gap, which means the client needs to start pre-fetching and buffering the next track before the current one finishes — tighter timing than YouTube's segment-by-segment adaptive fetch, where a brief stall is tolerable.</p>\n<p>Licensing here resembles Netflix more than YouTube: catalog availability is per-region, royalty accounting happens per stream, and offline downloads are DRM-wrapped and license-bounded exactly as in Netflix's model — just applied at the track level instead of the title level. Net effect: the storage/CDN engineering problem shrinks dramatically relative to video, while the recommendation problem gets finer-grained, and the licensing problem underneath looks like a track-level version of Netflix's.</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud</h2>\n<p><strong>Transcoding pipeline · Adaptive bitrate streaming (HLS/DASH) · Manifest file (segment list) · Candidate generation vs ranking · Collaborative filtering vs content-based filtering · Cold-start problem · Edge caching / CDN · Blob storage · Long-tail content · Open Connect-style proactive edge distribution · Pre-computed personalized rows · Gapless playback · DRM / licensing-bounded offline access · Hot-key write contention</strong></p>"}, {"num": 5, "title": "Geo Real-Time Dispatch", "subtitle": "Uber (flagship) · Food delivery apps", "tag": "Family D", "readMin": 8, "html": "<h1>Family D — Geo Real-Time Dispatch Systems</h1>\n<p>Dispatch systems come up in startup interviews because they compress three hard problems into one story: indexing and querying location data at scale, absorbing a write-heavy stream without drowning your primary database, and keeping two independent clients in sync on state that changes every few seconds. Nail this family and you can improvise on any \"track moving things and match them\" variant a startup throws at you — food delivery, field-service dispatch, fleet tracking, on-demand logistics — because the underlying primitives (geospatial index, location ingestion pipeline, matching loop, state machine) repeat with only the domain nouns swapped. Indian startups building hyperlocal delivery, logistics, and service marketplaces ask this literally, not as an abstraction exercise, so treat the Uber case study below as something you could be asked to whiteboard verbatim.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: Uber (Ride-Hailing)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining requirements</h3>\n<p>State functional requirements first, out loud, in this order: a rider can request a ride from point A to B and see an ETA; the system finds and assigns a nearby driver within seconds; both rider and driver see a live-updating trip (location, status) until drop-off; fare is calculated and charged at completion. Non-functional requirements are where interviewers actually score you: matching latency under ~5 seconds, high availability (a dispatch outage is revenue-zero, not just degraded), horizontal scalability by geography (a city in Texas and a city in Maharashtra don't need to talk to each other), eventual consistency acceptable for raw location data, but strong consistency required for trip state and payment. Explicitly say what's out of scope — driver onboarding/KYC, in-app chat, detailed route turn-by-turn — to bound the conversation; interviewers respect a candidate who scopes rather than one who tries to design everything.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p>Keep this to the handful of endpoints that matter: <code>POST /rides</code> (pickup, drop-off, vehicle type) returns a ride ID and estimated fare/ETA before confirmation; <code>POST /drivers/{id}/location</code> accepts high-frequency pings (lat, lng, heading, speed, timestamp) — this one endpoint is the highest-QPS thing in the whole system; <code>POST /drivers/{id}/respond</code> accepts/rejects a dispatch offer; <code>GET /rides/{id}</code> polls status as a fallback; and a persistent channel (WebSocket or long-lived push) streams driver location and trip-state changes to the rider app without polling. Naming the push channel explicitly is a signal you understand that REST alone can't deliver \"live tracking.\"</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating resources</h3>\n<p>Work the numbers instead of gesturing at \"a lot of traffic.\" Take one large metro: 2 million daily active riders, 100,000 drivers online at peak, pinging location every 4 seconds whether idle or on a trip. That's 100,000 ÷ 4 = 25,000 location writes/second for this one city alone, at roughly 100 bytes/ping (driver ID, lat, lng, heading, timestamp) — about 2.5 MB/s of inbound ingestion before you've matched a single ride. Compare that to ride-request volume: even a generous 2 million rides/day, with 15% of daily volume landing in the peak hour, is 300,000 requests/hour ≈ 83 requests/second. That gap — 25,000 writes/sec of location noise versus 83 requests/sec of actual business events — is the number you should say out loud, because it's the whole justification for treating location data differently from ride data. If you naively persisted every ping to your primary relational store, one city alone demands 25,000 sustained writes/sec; twenty cities would be 500,000 writes/sec, which no single Postgres instance survives. That's your setup for step 6.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling data</h3>\n<p>Split by durability and access pattern, not by \"put everything in one schema.\" Rider/driver profiles and ride records (fare, timestamps, rider_id, driver_id, state) live in a relational store (Postgres) — low-volume, need transactional guarantees, queried by ID. Live driver location is ephemeral and belongs in an in-memory geospatial index (Redis, using geohash-backed sorted sets, or an equivalent grid structure) with a TTL — a driver not heard from in ~30 seconds silently drops out of \"available\" results. Historical location trails (for ETA-model training, support disputes, fraud review) go to an append-only time-series store, written asynchronously and sampled down (e.g., one point every 15-20 seconds, not every raw ping).</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the high-level design</h3>\n<p>Rider and driver apps talk to an API gateway, which fronts a Ride/Trip service, a Location service, a Matching service, a Pricing service, and a Notification service. Location pings don't hit the Location service's database directly — they land on a message queue (Kafka, partitioned by city or geohash prefix) so bursts are absorbed instead of dropped. A consumer reads off that queue and updates the in-memory geo index synchronously (so matching sees fresh data) while asynchronously batching writes to durable storage. The Matching service queries the geo index (never the relational DB) for candidates, scores them, and hands a decision to the Trip service, which owns the state machine and fans out updates over the push channel. <div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>rider/driver apps → API gateway → {Ride, Location, Matching, Pricing, Notification} services, with Kafka sitting between location-ping ingestion and the in-memory geo index, and the geo index feeding Matching</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing components</h3>\n<p>This is where the five things interviewers actually probe live. <strong>Geospatial indexing</strong>: to answer \"find nearby drivers,\" you can't scan every driver's coordinates per request. Geohashing encodes lat/lng into a string where shared prefixes mean spatial proximity, so \"drivers near me\" becomes a prefix/range query over a sorted set (this is what Redis GEO commands do under the hood); a quadtree achieves the same goal by recursively subdividing the map into cells only where driver density requires it, giving more uniform query cost in sparse areas. Either way, say clearly: this index lives in memory, not in the primary DB. <strong>High-frequency location updates without overwhelming the database</strong>: the Kafka buffer plus in-memory index plus asynchronous, sampled persistence (step 3's 25,000 raw writes/sec becomes roughly 100,000 ÷ 15 ≈ 6,700 writes/sec to durable storage) is the full answer — say \"write batching\" and \"in-memory geo index\" explicitly. <strong>Matching</strong>: generate candidates by querying the geo index within an expanding radius (start at ~2 km, widen if fewer than 3 candidates), score by ETA from a routing estimate (not straight-line distance), driver rating, and acceptance likelihood, then dispatch to the top candidate with a ~10-second accept window before falling through to the next. Better systems batch requests in a hot zone for a second or two and solve a small assignment problem instead of greedily offering one at a time, to avoid two nearby riders fighting over the same driver. <strong>Dynamic/surge pricing</strong>: partition the city into a hex grid (H3 is the real-world tool name), compute a demand/supply ratio per cell every 1-2 minutes (open requests versus available drivers), map that ratio through a bounded multiplier function, and cache the result so pricing lookups are a fast read, not a live computation. <strong>Trip-state management</strong>: model the trip as an explicit state machine — requested → matched → driver_en_route → arrived → trip_started → completed — plus cancellation branches, persist every transition, and publish each change to both apps over the push channel rather than having clients poll.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing bottlenecks and scaling</h3>\n<p>Name the hot-zone/hotspot problem explicitly: a stadium letting out 50,000 people creates a geo-index hotspot and a matching-service surge in one small area while the rest of the city is quiet — mitigate with finer-grained partitioning in dense cells and backpressure on ping ingestion rather than uniform sharding. The database write bottleneck is already solved by the pipeline in step 6, but be ready to say why a naive design fails first — that contrast is what shows you understand the fix. Handle out-of-order or duplicate pings (mobile networks retry) with timestamp-based idempotency so a late-arriving stale ping doesn't overwrite a newer position. Partition the whole system by city/region so no service needs a global view, and treat a lost driver connection as a TTL expiry, not a crash.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Delta: Food Delivery (Zomato/Swiggy/DoorDash)</h2>\n<p>Same dispatch skeleton, three genuine differences worth naming precisely rather than hand-waving \"it's like Uber but for food.\" First, it's a three-sided marketplace, not two-sided: customer, restaurant, and delivery partner all have independent state and independent apps to update, so your trip-state machine gains a whole extra branch — order placed → accepted by restaurant → preparing → ready for pickup → picked up → delivered — and matching now has to consider restaurant readiness, not just rider-to-driver distance. Second, order-preparation-time prediction becomes a first-class estimation problem that ride-hailing doesn't have: you're not just estimating driving ETA, you're predicting how long a specific restaurant takes to cook a specific order (a regression problem fed by historical prep-time data per restaurant/item, updated continuously), and you deliberately delay dispatching a delivery partner until the food is close to ready, otherwise partners idle at the restaurant. Third, route optimization becomes multi-stop rather than point-to-point once you allow batched orders (one partner picking up from two restaurants or dropping to two customers), which turns matching into a lightweight vehicle-routing problem instead of a single-pair assignment. The real-time tracking UI also has a different shape: customers expect a stage-based progress bar (preparing → out for delivery → arriving) more than a live map in the early stages, because there's nothing moving to show until pickup happens. Everything else — geospatial indexing for nearby partners, high-frequency location pings, write batching, surge/demand pricing on delivery fees — carries over unchanged from the Uber case study.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong>\n- Geohashing\n- Quadtree\n- Hot-zone / hotspot problem\n- Write batching\n- In-memory geo index\n- Candidate generation / expanding-radius search\n- Bipartite matching (batch dispatch)\n- H3 hex-grid partitioning\n- Demand/supply ratio (surge pricing)\n- Trip-state machine\n- TTL-based freshness\n- Three-sided marketplace</p>"}, {"num": 6, "title": "E-Commerce Marketplace", "subtitle": "Amazon (flagship) · Flipkart / Shopify", "tag": "Family E", "readMin": 7, "html": "<h1>Family E — E-Commerce Marketplace Systems</h1>\n<p>E-commerce system design questions test a different muscle than dispatch: not \"can you track moving things\" but \"can you keep money and inventory correct under concurrent access while still being fast at read-heavy catalog scale.\" It's the archetype interviewers use to check whether you know optimistic vs pessimistic locking, why a naive \"check stock then decrement\" pattern breaks at scale, and how to model a multi-step business process (order fulfillment) as an explicit state machine rather than a status string you mutate in place. Startups building any transactional marketplace — retail, B2B ordering, ticketing — reuse this exact skeleton, so it's worth memorizing precisely rather than reasoning it out live under pressure.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: Amazon (E-Commerce Platform)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional: browse/search a catalog, view product detail with live price and stock, add to cart, check out (address, payment, review), track an order through fulfillment. Non-functional: search and browse must be fast and highly available even if slightly stale (a product page showing \"in stock\" a few seconds out of date is fine); checkout must be strongly consistent for inventory and payment — you cannot sell the same unit twice; the system must survive extreme, short-lived traffic spikes (flash sales) without falling over, and must scale horizontally with catalog size and seller count. Say explicitly what's out of scope for the round: recommendations/personalization ranking, returns/refunds workflow, tax calculation — these are real systems but not what's being tested here.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p><code>GET /products/search?q=</code> hits a search index, not the primary DB. <code>GET /products/{id}</code> returns catalog detail plus a fast, separately-cached stock indicator. <code>POST /cart/items</code> and <code>DELETE /cart/items/{id}</code> mutate a per-user cart. <code>POST /orders</code> is the critical endpoint: it takes a cart snapshot and payment method, and internally must atomically reserve inventory, charge payment, and create the order record — say out loud that this single endpoint is where all your consistency guarantees have to hold. <code>GET /orders/{id}</code> returns fulfillment status for tracking.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating resources</h3>\n<p>Ground this in a flash-sale scenario, since that's the standard follow-up. Take a marketplace with 300 million active SKUs. During a flash sale's first hour, assume 50 million visitors hit the site, with a 2% visitor-to-checkout-attempt conversion rate: 50,000,000 × 0.02 = 1,000,000 checkout attempts in that hour, averaging 1,000,000 ÷ 3,600 ≈ 280 checkout QPS — but traffic isn't smooth, it spikes around the top of the hour, so plan for roughly 10x that instantaneous rate: ~2,800 checkout QPS at peak. Compare that to browse/search traffic, which runs one to two orders of magnitude higher (tens of thousands of QPS) because every checkout is preceded by dozens of product views — this is why catalog search and checkout are architected as entirely separate systems with different consistency needs. Now attach the number that actually matters for step 6: if a single flash-sale SKU has 500 units in stock and even a fraction of that 2,800 checkout QPS — say 5,000 attempts in the first two seconds — targets that one SKU, a naive \"read stock, then write stock-1\" pattern will oversell by hundreds of units before anyone notices, because reads and writes aren't atomic against each other.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling data</h3>\n<p>Catalog data (title, description, images, category) is read-heavy and eventually-consistent-tolerant, so it lives in a document or relational store behind a search index (Elasticsearch/OpenSearch) built from it — search never queries the source-of-truth DB directly. Inventory is its own service with its own store, keyed by SKU, because it needs the strongest consistency guarantees in the whole system and the highest write contention on hot items. Cart is a separate, user-scoped, low-durability-requirement store (Redis or a fast key-value store is fine — losing an abandoned cart is not a business emergency). Orders are relational, with a strict schema (order_id, user_id, line items, state, timestamps, payment reference) because they need transactional guarantees and are audited.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the high-level design</h3>\n<p>Client apps hit an API gateway fronting a Catalog/Search service, a Cart service, an Inventory service, an Order service, and a Payment service (often a third-party integration, covered in the Payment Systems module). Search reads flow: client → search index (denormalized, rebuilt async from catalog changes) → response, never touching Inventory. Checkout flow: client → Order service → (Inventory service to reserve stock) + (Payment service to charge) → on both succeeding, Order service commits the order and publishes an event for downstream fulfillment/notification services to consume. <div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>checkout path fans out from Order service to Inventory (reservation) and Payment (charge) as near-atomic steps, with an event bus feeding fulfillment/notifications after commit</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing components</h3>\n<p><strong>Inventory and the overselling problem</strong> is the centerpiece. Pessimistic locking (row-level lock on the SKU's stock row for the duration of the check-and-decrement) guarantees correctness but serializes every checkout attempt on a hot SKU, which is exactly the failure mode step 3's numbers describe. Optimistic locking (read the stock value with a version number, attempt to write <code>stock = stock - 1 WHERE version = read_version</code>, retry on conflict) scales better under contention because it doesn't hold a lock while waiting, but under extreme contention (thousands of retries per second on one row) it can thrash. The pattern that actually survives flash-sale load is <strong>inventory reservation</strong>: an atomic decrement operation (a single <code>UPDATE ... WHERE stock &gt; 0</code> or an atomic counter in a fast store) reserves a unit the instant checkout starts, with a short TTL — if payment doesn't complete within that window, the reservation is released back to available stock. This converts \"check then act\" into a single atomic operation, which is the whole fix. <strong>Cart service</strong> is deliberately kept simple and eventually consistent — it's a convenience layer, not a source of truth, and price/stock are re-validated at checkout time regardless of what the cart cached, because prices and stock can change between add-to-cart and purchase. <strong>Order fulfillment as a state machine</strong>: created → payment_authorized → inventory_reserved → confirmed → shipped → delivered, plus cancelled/refunded branches, each transition persisted and event-driven so warehouse, shipping, and notification systems react independently rather than the Order service calling each of them synchronously.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing bottlenecks and scaling</h3>\n<p>Flash-sale traffic is a thundering-herd problem: put a queue in front of checkout for the hottest SKUs (accept the request, return \"processing,\" and drain the queue against the reservation counter) rather than letting every request hit Inventory synchronously — this bounds worst-case load to what your reservation store can sustain instead of whatever the internet sends. Cache aggressively on the read path (product pages, search results) with short TTLs so catalog reads never compete with checkout writes for the same database. Shard inventory by SKU so one hot item's contention doesn't degrade unrelated items. Separate the read replicas serving catalog/search from the primary handling inventory writes, and keep the inventory reservation counter in a low-latency store (Redis atomic increment/decrement, or an in-memory counter service) rather than a full relational transaction on the hot path.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Delta: Multi-Seller Marketplace (Flipkart/Shopify)</h2>\n<p>The core checkout/inventory machinery is identical; what changes is that \"the seller\" is no longer Amazon itself but one of potentially millions of independent parties, and that reshapes four things. First, <strong>product information management (PIM)</strong> becomes genuinely harder: the same physical product can be listed by multiple sellers with different prices, condition, and stock, so the catalog needs a canonical product entity with seller-specific \"offers\" attached to it (this is the buy-box problem — deciding which seller's offer to show as default). Second, <strong>inventory is per-seller, not global</strong>: the Inventory service partitions by seller_id in addition to SKU, and a single order can span multiple sellers, meaning fulfillment for one order might split into multiple independent shipments each tracked separately. Third, <strong>payments require settlement splitting</strong>: the platform collects one payment from the buyer but owes each seller their share minus commission, so you need a ledger-style settlement system (previewed properly in the Payment Systems module) that reconciles marketplace fees, refunds, and payouts on a schedule rather than an instant pass-through charge. Fourth, sellers need their own <strong>dashboards and analytics</strong> — order volume, inventory levels, payout history — which means the platform exposes a whole second API surface and set of read models scoped to seller_id, separate from the buyer-facing catalog and checkout APIs. Everything else — the reservation pattern for avoiding overselling, the order state machine, search-index-separate-from-source-of-truth — carries over from the Amazon case study unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong>\n- Optimistic locking\n- Pessimistic locking\n- Inventory reservation\n- Race condition / overselling\n- Order state machine\n- Thundering herd\n- Buy-box problem\n- Seller-scoped inventory partitioning\n- Settlement splitting\n- Eventual consistency (catalog) vs strong consistency (checkout)\n- Read replica / write primary separation\n- Event-driven fulfillment</p>"}, {"num": 7, "title": "Payment Systems", "subtitle": "Stripe (flagship) · PayPal", "tag": "Family F", "readMin": 8, "html": "<h1>Family F — Payment Systems</h1>\n<p>Payment systems are the highest-stakes family in this course because correctness isn't a nice-to-have — a bug here means either a customer is charged twice or a merchant never gets paid, and startups building or integrating payments (which is most fintech-adjacent Indian startups right now) will specifically probe whether you understand why payment systems are architected so conservatively. This is also where interviewers separate candidates who've only built CRUD apps from those who've thought about failure modes: idempotency, atomicity across services, and immutable audit trails aren't academic here, they're the difference between a system that survives a network retry and one that silently double-charges someone. Expect this family to come up directly if you're interviewing anywhere near checkout, billing, or wallets — and even in unrelated rounds, saying \"idempotency key\" unprompted signals seniority.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: Stripe (Payment Gateway / API-First Processor)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional: a merchant's server calls an API to charge a customer's card, receives a definitive success/failure result, and gets notified asynchronously of later status changes (a charge that settles, a dispute, a refund). Non-functional — say these first, because they define the whole system: every write must be safe to retry (a merchant's network can time out after the charge actually succeeded, and they will retry), money movements must be auditable and reconstructable after the fact, the system must never lose a transaction record even under partial failure, and it must be fast enough for real-time checkout (sub-second response) while running fraud checks in that same window. Out of scope for this round: card-network/bank integration internals, tax handling, currency conversion mechanics.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p><code>POST /charges</code> with an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header (client-generated, e.g. a UUID tied to this specific checkout attempt) is the single most important API detail in this whole module — say it explicitly, don't let it slide by as a minor header. <code>POST /refunds</code>, <code>GET /charges/{id}</code> for status, and <code>POST /payment_methods</code> to tokenize a card (returns an opaque token, never the raw PAN, to the merchant). Webhooks are the other half of the API surface: the merchant registers an endpoint, and Stripe pushes events (<code>charge.succeeded</code>, <code>charge.failed</code>, <code>charge.disputed</code>) to it asynchronously, because some payment outcomes (bank confirmation, fraud review) aren't known at the moment the API call returns.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating resources</h3>\n<p>Take a mid-size processor handling 10 million transactions/day: 10,000,000 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 116 transactions/second average. Payment traffic is lumpy around retail events, so plan for a 15-20x burst multiplier on peak days — call it roughly 2,000-2,300 transactions/second at peak. Each transaction isn't one write — it's several: an idempotency-key lookup, a ledger entry (at least two rows, debit and credit — more in step 4), a fraud-score computation, and a webhook dispatch, so the actual internal operation count per second is more like 5-6x the raw transaction number, i.e. 10,000-14,000 internal writes/sec at peak. That multiplier is the number to say out loud when asked \"so how many transactions per second does your database actually see\" — it's a common follow-up trap for candidates who only count the customer-facing charge.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling data</h3>\n<p>This is the conceptual core of the whole module. Payment systems do not use a single mutable <code>balance</code> column that you increment and decrement — a balance field can be corrupted by a missed update, a double-processed retry, or a race condition, and you'd have no way to reconstruct how it got wrong. Instead, use a <strong>double-entry ledger</strong>: every transaction is recorded as at least two immutable rows — a debit to one account and an equal credit to another (e.g., debit customer's card, credit merchant's payable account, with platform fee as a third entry) — and any balance is a <em>derived</em> sum over ledger entries, never stored as the source of truth. This makes the system append-only and auditable: nothing is ever updated or deleted, only new entries are added (a refund is a new reversing entry, not a mutation of the original charge). The <code>Idempotency-Key</code> from step 2 is stored alongside the resulting charge record specifically so a retried request with the same key returns the original result instead of creating a second ledger entry.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the high-level design</h3>\n<p>A Charge API service sits in front of an Idempotency service (a fast key-value lookup checked before any processing begins), a Ledger service (append-only, the system of record), a Fraud service (scores the transaction inline, sub-second budget), a Payment Network Gateway (talks to card networks/banks), and a Webhook Delivery service (async, with retry). The critical-path flow: request arrives → idempotency check → fraud score → call out to the card network → on success, write the ledger entry → return response to merchant → asynchronously enqueue webhook events for anyone subscribed. <div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>Charge API → Idempotency check → Fraud scoring → Card network call → Ledger write, with Webhook Delivery branching off asynchronously after the ledger write</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing components</h3>\n<p><strong>Idempotency keys</strong> are non-negotiable because HTTP retries are a fact of life over the internet — a merchant's request can succeed on Stripe's side but time out on the merchant's side before they see the response, and without a dedup key their automatic retry creates a second real charge; the fix is to hash/store the key with the eventual result for a window (e.g., 24 hours) and short-circuit any repeat. <strong>The ledger/double-entry model</strong> was covered in step 4 — the interview-ready line is \"balances are computed, never stored, because a derived value from immutable history can always be reconstructed and audited, while a mutable field can silently drift.\" <strong>Distributed transaction handling</strong>: a single charge touches multiple systems (fraud, card network, ledger, notifications) that can't share one database transaction, so instead of two-phase commit (which blocks and doesn't survive a coordinator failure well) use a <strong>saga</strong>: each step is a local transaction that, on failure, triggers a compensating action for the steps already completed (e.g., if the ledger write fails after the card network already approved the charge, a compensating reversal/void is issued to the network rather than leaving money in limbo). <strong>Fraud detection</strong> runs a fast rules engine first (velocity checks — too many attempts from one card/IP in a short window, mismatched billing details) for cheap, sub-millisecond rejection of obvious abuse, then an ML scoring model for the harder cases, all within the sub-second budget from step 1 — say explicitly that rules and ML are layered, not either/or. <strong>PCI compliance and tokenization</strong>: raw card numbers are never stored in your own database — the card is exchanged once for an opaque token via a PCI-compliant vault, and every subsequent operation uses the token, which is what keeps your systems out of the most expensive PCI compliance tier. <strong>Webhook delivery</strong> must survive the merchant's endpoint being down: queue events, retry with exponential backoff, and let the merchant reconcile via a <code>GET</code> endpoint if they suspect a missed event, since at-least-once delivery means merchants must also handle duplicate webhook events idempotently on their side.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing bottlenecks and scaling</h3>\n<p>The idempotency-key lookup is on the critical path of every single request, so it needs to be a low-latency store (in-memory/KV, not a relational table with heavy locking) or it becomes the bottleneck for the entire 2,000+ TPS peak load from step 3. The ledger is append-only and write-heavy but never needs in-place updates, which makes it a good fit for horizontal partitioning by account or time range without worrying about update conflicts. Fraud scoring is the one synchronous step with real computational cost (an ML inference call) sitting inside your latency budget — mitigate by running the rules engine first to filter out easy cases before hitting the model, and by pre-computing risk signals (device fingerprint history, velocity counters) asynchronously ahead of time rather than at request time. Webhook delivery scales by being fully decoupled (a queue plus workers) so a slow or dead merchant endpoint never backs up the payment critical path.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Delta: PayPal (Consumer Wallet Model)</h2>\n<p>The ledger, idempotency, and fraud-detection machinery underneath is largely the same; the real difference is the product model on top. Stripe is API-first and pass-through: it processes a charge from a customer's card to a merchant's bank account and mostly doesn't hold funds. PayPal is fundamentally a <strong>consumer wallet with a stored balance</strong> — users load money in, hold a balance, and pay out of that balance or a linked card/bank as a fallback — so PayPal's ledger has an extra durable \"account balance\" concept per user that Stripe's merchant-facing API mostly abstracts away; note this doesn't contradict step 4's \"never store balance as a mutable field\" principle, it's still derived from ledger entries, just derived <em>per user account</em> rather than per merchant settlement. Second, because PayPal sits directly in consumer-to-consumer and consumer-to-merchant transactions, it owns <strong>buyer/seller protection and dispute resolution</strong> as a first-class workflow — a formal claims process with holds on funds, evidence submission, and arbitration — a heavier state machine than Stripe's simpler dispute-forwarding-to-card-network model. Third, PayPal does more of its own <strong>cross-border currency handling</strong> directly (holding multi-currency balances, converting at its own rates) rather than delegating that entirely to card networks and issuing banks, which means its ledger needs a currency dimension on every entry and a conversion-rate audit trail, not just a single-currency debit/credit pair.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong>\n- Idempotency key\n- Double-entry ledger\n- Append-only / immutable audit trail\n- Saga pattern\n- Two-phase commit (2PC)\n- Compensating transaction\n- Tokenization\n- PCI compliance\n- Webhook (at-least-once delivery)\n- Rules engine + ML scoring pipeline\n- Velocity checks\n- Settlement vs stored-balance model</p>"}, {"num": 8, "title": "File Storage & Sync", "subtitle": "Dropbox (flagship) · Google Drive", "tag": "Family G", "readMin": 8, "html": "<h1>Family G — File Storage &amp; Sync Systems</h1>\n<p>File sync systems test whether you can separate concerns that look similar but scale completely differently — file content (huge, changes rarely, needs cheap storage) versus file metadata (small, changes constantly, needs fast consistent queries) — and whether you know the actual mechanism for \"only re-upload what changed\" instead of just saying \"diffing\" vaguely. It's also one of the few families where conflict resolution is a real, nameable design decision (last-write-wins vs version vectors vs manual conflict copies) rather than an afterthought, which is exactly the kind of precision interviewers listen for. Any startup building document collaboration, backup, or asset-management tooling draws on this same skeleton, so it generalizes well beyond literal \"build Dropbox\" prompts.</p>\n<h2>Flagship Case Study: Dropbox (File Storage &amp; Sync)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional: a user can add/edit/delete files in a local synced folder and have those changes propagate to all their other devices and to anyone they've shared the folder with; the system must handle offline edits and reconcile them on reconnect; version history should be recoverable. Non-functional: sync should be fast for small edits to large files (you shouldn't re-upload a whole 2GB file because one paragraph in one document changed), storage must scale to hundreds of millions of users' files cheaply, metadata operations (listing a folder, checking what changed) must be low-latency even though blob storage itself can be higher-latency, and the system must tolerate intermittent connectivity gracefully. Out of scope: full real-time collaborative co-editing (that's the sibling delta), granular permission management details, desktop-client internals.</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p><code>POST /files/{path}/blocks</code> — uploads only the changed blocks of a file (not the whole file), referencing a manifest of block hashes. <code>GET /files/{path}/metadata</code> returns version, size, last-modified, and the list of block hashes that make up the current version — the client diffs this against its local manifest to know what to fetch or push. <code>GET /delta</code> (or an equivalent \"what changed since cursor X\" endpoint) is what drives sync — the client doesn't ask \"give me everything,\" it asks \"what changed since my last known state,\" and a long-lived connection (WebSocket, or long-poll as fallback) pushes a lightweight \"something changed, call /delta\" signal so clients don't have to poll aggressively.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating resources</h3>\n<p>Take 500 million registered users, 50 million daily active, averaging 5 GB of actively-synced storage each — that's already 250 petabytes of data in motion for actives alone, before counting inactive accounts' cold storage, which is why blob storage has to be cheap, commodity object storage, not a database. For sync volume: 50 million DAU each generating roughly 20 file-change events/day (edits, new files, deletes) is 50,000,000 × 20 = 1,000,000,000 sync events/day, or 1,000,000,000 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 11,600 events/second on average — and since usage clusters around working hours, plan for a 3x evening/workday peak, roughly 35,000 events/second. Now the number that actually justifies chunking: if a user edits one paragraph in a 100 MB document and the system re-uploads the whole file, that's 100 MB moved for a one-line change; split into 4 MB blocks (a realistic block size), the same edit touches maybe 1-2 blocks — 4-8 MB moved instead of 100 MB, a 12-25x reduction in bandwidth and storage churn. At 35,000 sync events/second peak, that ratio is the difference between a system that's feasible and one that isn't.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling data</h3>\n<p>Split hard into two systems with different scaling shapes. <strong>Blob storage</strong> holds the actual file content, addressed by the content's hash rather than a filename or path (content-addressed storage) — this enables deduplication for free: if two users (or the same user in two places) upload a block with an identical hash, it's stored once and referenced twice, and it's cheap, high-latency-tolerant, horizontally scalable object storage (think S3-equivalent). <strong>Metadata service</strong> is a separate, low-latency, strongly-consistent store holding the file/folder tree, per-file version history, and the ordered list of block hashes that reconstruct each version — this answers \"what changed\" and \"what does this file currently look like\" fast, without ever touching blob storage for a simple listing or status check.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the high-level design</h3>\n<p>Desktop/mobile clients talk to a Sync API service, backed by the Metadata service (file tree, versions) and a separate Block/Blob service (content-addressed storage plus a Chunking service that splits files into blocks and computes hashes client-side before upload). A Notification service maintains long-lived connections (WebSocket, falling back to long-poll) to all of a user's active devices and pushes a lightweight \"your data changed, call /delta\" signal whenever the Metadata service records a change, so devices don't poll. <div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>client → Chunking (local) → Sync API → {Metadata service (file tree/versions), Block service (content-addressed blob storage)}, with Notification service pushing change signals to all of a user's other devices</div></p>\n<h3>6. Detailing components</h3>\n<p><strong>Chunking and deduplication</strong>: the client splits files into fixed- or content-defined blocks (roughly 4 MB each), hashes each block, and only uploads blocks whose hash isn't already known to the server — this is <strong>content-addressed storage</strong>, and it gives you both efficient re-sync (only changed blocks move) and cross-user deduplication (two users with the same file, or the same user syncing across devices, share the same stored blocks) essentially for free. <strong>Metadata vs blob separation</strong>, already covered in step 4, is worth restating here as the answer to \"why not just store files in a database\" — metadata queries need to be fast and consistent at high volume, blob storage needs to be cheap at massive scale, and conflating them forces you into the worse trade-off on both sides. <strong>Sync protocol and conflict resolution</strong>: when two devices edit the same file offline and both reconnect, you need an explicit policy, and there are three real options to name — <strong>last-write-wins</strong> (simplest, but silently discards one user's edit, acceptable for low-stakes settings data but not for documents); <strong>version vectors</strong> (each device tracks a vector of {device: version} pairs, so the system can detect true concurrent edits versus a clean linear history and knows when a real conflict — not just a delay — occurred); and <strong>manual conflict copies</strong> (the real-world default for a pure file-sync product: when a true conflict is detected, keep both versions as separate files, e.g. <code>document (conflicted copy from Amey's laptop).docx</code>, and let the human resolve it) — say explicitly that this third option is favored because silently merging arbitrary binary files isn't safely automatable, unlike structured text. <strong>Notification mechanism</strong>: a long-lived WebSocket per active device is the efficient version; long-polling is the fallback for restrictive networks; either way the push payload is intentionally tiny (just \"something changed, go call /delta\") so the expensive work of figuring out <em>what</em> changed stays in the Metadata service's <code>/delta</code> endpoint, not the push channel itself.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing bottlenecks and scaling</h3>\n<p>The Metadata service is the hot path for almost every operation (even a blob upload needs a metadata write to register the new version) and needs to scale independently of blob storage — shard it by user/account ID so one user's activity never contends with another's. Blob storage scales horizontally by design (commodity object storage) but benefits enormously from deduplication reducing actual bytes stored — at 250+ PB of nominal data, even modest dedup rates save real money. The Notification service holding millions of concurrent long-lived connections is a connection-scaling problem distinct from the data-scaling problem elsewhere in the system — partition connections across many notification servers keyed by user ID, and treat a dropped connection as \"client will reconnect and call /delta to catch up,\" not as a delivery guarantee you must retry indefinitely. For very large files or very active accounts, chunking bounds worst-case re-sync cost regardless of file size, which is the whole point of not treating \"file\" as the unit of sync.</p>\n<h2>Sibling Delta: Google Drive (Real-Time Collaborative Editing)</h2>\n<p>The storage, chunking, and metadata layers are effectively the same problem Dropbox solves; the genuine difference is that Google Drive layers <strong>real-time collaborative editing</strong> on top, which changes the sync unit from \"whole file/block\" to \"individual keystroke-level operation\" for its native document types (Docs, Sheets). Instead of syncing a file and resolving conflicts after the fact, concurrent edits are handled live via <strong>operational transforms (OT)</strong> or <strong>CRDTs (conflict-free replicated data types)</strong>: every keystroke is an operation broadcast to all active collaborators, and OT mathematically transforms incoming operations against ones already applied locally so every client converges to the same final document without ever needing a \"conflicted copy\" fallback; CRDTs achieve the same convergence guarantee with a data structure designed so operations commute and merge deterministically regardless of arrival order, which is easier to reason about correctness for but historically has had more storage/complexity overhead per edit. This only applies to native structured document formats — for opaque binary files (PDFs, images, arbitrary uploads) Drive falls back to the same block-level, conflicted-copy sync model as Dropbox, because you can't operationally-transform a JPEG. The other consequence is architectural: collaborative editing needs a stateful, low-latency real-time layer (an active \"document session\" server holding in-memory operation history for documents currently being co-edited) that Dropbox's pure file-sync model never needs, since it has no concept of two people editing the same byte range simultaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong>\n- Content-addressed storage\n- Chunking / block-level diffing\n- Deduplication\n- Metadata service vs blob storage separation\n- Delta sync (\"what changed since X\")\n- Last-write-wins\n- Version vector\n- Conflicted copy\n- Long-poll vs WebSocket push\n- Operational transform (OT)\n- CRDT (conflict-free replicated data type)\n- Convergence guarantee</p>"}, {"num": 9, "title": "Search Systems", "subtitle": "Google Search (flagship)", "tag": "Family H", "readMin": 6, "html": "<h1>Family H — Search Systems</h1>\n<p>Search and indexing show up in interviews far beyond literal search-engine companies, because any product with search-over-content — e-commerce product search, log/observability search, in-app document or message search, code search — needs the same underlying primitives: an inverted index, a ranking function, and a query-serving path that stays fast as the corpus grows. Google Search is the flagship because it has the most extreme version of every sub-problem (crawl scale, index size, ranking complexity, query volume), so once you can narrate it end-to-end, you can scale the same ideas down to a product catalog or a log search feature. There's no sibling case study in this family — go deep here.</p>\n<h2>Google Search (Search Engine)</h2>\n<h3>1. Defining Requirements</h3>\n<p>Functional: crawl the web, build a searchable index, accept a text query and return ranked, relevant results within a fraction of a second, keep results fresh as pages change. Non-functional: extremely read-heavy (billions of queries/day vs. a much smaller rate of index updates), very low query latency (sub-200ms end-to-end is the practical bar), high availability, eventual consistency is fine for the index (a page update doesn't need to be searchable within milliseconds). Clarifying questions: are we building crawling + indexing + serving, or just the query-serving path against an existing index? What freshness SLA (news vs. static pages)? What's an acceptable staleness window? Is personalization in scope, or pure relevance ranking? What's the target corpus size?</p>\n<h3>2. Developing APIs</h3>\n<p>The externally visible surface is tiny: <code>GET /search?q=&lt;query&gt;&amp;page=&lt;n&gt;</code> returning a ranked list of {url, title, snippet}. The real API design work here is internal, service-to-service: a <strong>query-serving API</strong> (query → parsed/normalized terms), an <strong>index-lookup API</strong> (terms → matching document IDs + scores, likely gRPC internally between the query layer and hundreds of index shards), and a <strong>ranking API</strong> (candidate doc IDs → final ordered list). This is a clean example of \"internal, latency-sensitive, strongly-typed, so gRPC over REST\" — say that explicitly.</p>\n<h3>3. Estimating Resources</h3>\n<p>Assume ~5 billion searches/day → QPS ≈ 5,000,000,000 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 58,000/sec average, ~150-200k/sec peak. Corpus: assume ~50 billion indexed pages, average parsed page ≈ 20KB → raw crawled text ≈ ~1 exabyte uncompressed. The inverted index itself (postings lists, not raw pages) is far smaller than the raw corpus after compression, but still reaches tens of petabytes at this scale — which is why it's sharded across thousands of machines rather than treated as a single-node problem at all. Crawl bandwidth: keeping even a modest freshness window means re-fetching billions of pages/day; at ~20KB/page that's tens of terabytes/day of inbound crawl bandwidth, separate from serving traffic. The number to say out loud: at this scale, no single machine holds the index — everything downstream of this step is about sharding and parallel fan-out.</p>\n<h3>4. Modeling Data</h3>\n<p>Two very different data shapes live here, and conflating them is the most common interview mistake. The <strong>crawl store</strong> (raw fetched pages + metadata like last-crawled time, checksum for dedup) is naturally document-shaped — a document/wide-column store keyed by URL-hash fits well. The <strong>inverted index</strong> (term → list of document IDs containing that term, with position/frequency data for ranking) is a specialized structure, not a general-purpose database — it's built and stored as sharded, sorted postings lists, partitioned by term range or document range across index shards, optimized purely for one access pattern: \"give me all docs containing term X, fast.\" Don't force the inverted index into a \"SQL vs. NoSQL\" framing — say explicitly it's a custom-built structure; that signals real understanding.</p>\n<h3>5. Creating the High-Level Design</h3>\n<p><div class=\"diagram-note\"><span class=\"diagram-note-label\">Diagram</span>Offline pipeline: Crawler → Dedup/Content Store → Indexer (builds sharded inverted index). Online pipeline: Client → LB → Query Layer → fan-out to Index Shards → Ranker → Result Cache → Client</div></p>\n<p>There are genuinely two pipelines here, and saying so out loud is itself a scoring signal. The <strong>offline/indexing pipeline</strong>: crawler fetches pages → a dedup/content-processing stage strips duplicates and boilerplate → an indexer builds/updates the sharded inverted index. The <strong>online/query-serving pipeline</strong>: a client query hits a load balancer, a query layer parses/normalizes it, fans the query out in parallel to every relevant index shard, gathers candidate documents back, hands them to a ranking stage, and caches the final result for repeat queries before returning it.</p>\n<h3>6. Detailing Components</h3>\n<p>Two components earn a real deep-dive here — pick both, but keep each tight, and explicitly say you're treating crawler mechanics and storage format as \"solved problems\" for this conversation.</p>\n<p><strong>Crawling at scale.</strong> Three sub-problems: <strong>politeness</strong> (rate-limit requests per domain so you don't overload the sites you're crawling — respect robots.txt and per-host concurrency limits), <strong>freshness</strong> (recrawl frequency should match how often a page actually changes — a news homepage gets recrawled far more often than a static about-page, typically decided by tracking each URL's historical change rate), and <strong>dedup</strong> (compute a content hash/fingerprint per page and skip re-indexing near-identical content, which also catches mirrored or scraped pages).</p>\n<p><strong>Ranking.</strong> Two layers: a <strong>link-analysis signal</strong> (PageRank-style — a page's importance is a function of how many other important pages link to it, computed offline as a graph algorithm over the whole web graph, not at query time) combined with <strong>query-time relevance signals</strong> (term frequency/matching, proximity of query terms on the page, freshness, and — outside pure text — click-through and engagement signals). The interview-safe framing: link analysis gives you a static, query-independent \"importance\" score per page; relevance scoring combines that with query-specific matching at serve time to produce the final order.</p>\n<h3>7. Addressing Bottlenecks and Scaling</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sharded index / query fan-out</strong> — the index is split across thousands of shards; a query hits all relevant shards in parallel and results are merged, so latency is bounded by the slowest shard, not total index size.</li>\n<li><strong>Caching</strong> — cache full result pages for popular/repeated queries (a small fraction of distinct queries drive a large fraction of traffic), and cache partial postings-list lookups for common terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Read replicas of index shards</strong> — each shard is replicated so query fan-out isn't bottlenecked on a single copy, and replicas double as failover.</li>\n<li><strong>Async/offline indexing</strong> — index updates never block query serving; the crawl-and-index pipeline runs fully decoupled from the query-serving path, connected only by the index being swapped in once built or updated incrementally.</li>\n<li><strong>Geographic distribution / edge serving</strong> — query layers and cached results are placed close to users to cut network round-trip latency, the same principle as a CDN but for compute rather than static assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Circuit breakers/timeouts on fan-out</strong> — if one index shard is slow or down, the query layer returns partial results within a deadline rather than waiting indefinitely, trading completeness for latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary you must be able to say out loud:</strong> inverted index, crawl frontier, politeness policy, freshness/recrawl rate, content dedup/fingerprinting, PageRank / link analysis, relevance scoring, query fan-out, sharded index, postings list, tf-idf-style term matching, result caching.</p>"}, {"num": 10, "title": "Combining Patterns", "subtitle": "Decomposing prompts you haven't seen before", "tag": "Synthesis", "readMin": 4, "html": "<h1>Combining Patterns — When the Prompt Isn't One of the Sixteen</h1>\n<p>Here's the thing CodeKredos was right about but didn't say out loud: no startup interviewer is going to ask you to \"design Twitter.\" They're going to ask you something that doesn't map cleanly onto any single case study — \"design our internal support ticket system,\" \"design a notification service for our app,\" \"design a leaderboard for our game,\" \"how would you build the feature we just described.\" If Weeks 2-9 taught you eight named systems, this week teaches you the actual transferable skill: decomposing an unfamiliar prompt into the families you already know, on the spot.</p>\n<h2>The decomposition move</h2>\n<p>When you hear an unfamiliar prompt, your first job isn't to panic that you haven't seen it before — it's to ask \"which of the eight families does each <em>piece</em> of this borrow from?\" Most real prompts are two or three families stitched together, not a ninth new thing.</p>\n<p>Worked examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Design an internal support ticket system\"</strong> → this is E-commerce's <strong>order state machine</strong> (created → assigned → in_progress → resolved → closed, exactly the same shape as an order) plus Messaging's <strong>notification/presence</strong> pattern (notify an agent when a ticket is assigned, notify a customer when it's updated) plus Search's <strong>full-text search over tickets</strong>. Three families, zero new ideas.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Design a leaderboard for our game\"</strong> → this is Social Feed &amp; Graph's <strong>fan-out and caching</strong> logic (a leaderboard is just a ranked, frequently-read, infrequently-written feed) combined with Payments' <strong>eventual-consistency-is-fine-here vs strong-consistency-matters-there</strong> judgment call (score updates can lag a few seconds; the <em>user's own</em> score after they submit should feel instant — that's a cache-then-reconcile pattern straight out of Week 2's timeline cache).</li>\n<li><strong>\"Design a notification system\"</strong> (push + email + SMS fan-out) → almost entirely Messaging's <strong>fan-out-on-write plus offline store-and-forward</strong> pattern, with the \"device\" swapped for \"channel\" — a user has multiple notification channels the way a WhatsApp group has multiple members.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Design a URL shortener\"</strong> → this looks like nothing you've studied, and that's the point: it's mostly Week 1's raw estimation skill (how many redirects/sec, how big is the key space) plus a tiny sliver of Search's key-lookup pattern (short code → long URL is a single-key lookup, not an inverted index) plus Week 6's caching-the-hot-path instinct (a small fraction of shortened URLs get the overwhelming majority of clicks — cache those). There is no \"URL shortener family\" in this course because there doesn't need to be one; you already own every piece.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Say this decomposition out loud in the actual interview: \"This looks like it borrows the state-machine idea from order fulfillment and the notification fan-out idea from messaging systems.\" That sentence alone tells the interviewer you're pattern-matching, not guessing — which is a stronger signal than accidentally landing on the right architecture.</p>\n<h2>Designing a feature of your own future product</h2>\n<p>The other version of this — and the one CodeKredos flagged as your real growth path, not just an interview trick — is being handed a genuinely new product idea and asked to design it from scratch, or catching yourself needing this at your own company as you build toward the in-house job tracker replacing World Shiner. The process is identical to the 7-step framework, with one addition at the front: <strong>before step 1, write one sentence stating what the product actually does</strong>, because everything else — your clarifying questions, your API surface, your estimates — is downstream of that sentence being right. A one-sentence product statement you can defend is worth more than a beautiful diagram for the wrong product.</p>\n<h2>The full vocabulary cheat-sheet (Weeks 1-9, consolidated)</h2>\n<p>Skim this the night before an interview — if a term here doesn't immediately trigger a one-sentence definition in your head, go back to the week it came from.</p>\n<p><strong>Framework &amp; estimation:</strong> functional vs. non-functional requirements, back-of-envelope estimation, p99 latency, read:write ratio, denormalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Data &amp; consistency:</strong> wide-column store, document store, graph store, eventual consistency, strong consistency, optimistic locking, pessimistic locking, double-entry ledger, append-only/immutable audit trail, version vector, CRDT, operational transform.</p>\n<p><strong>Caching &amp; scaling:</strong> cache-aside, write-through, write-back, horizontal sharding, consistent hashing, read replica, CDN, edge caching, connection pooling, circuit breaker, thundering herd.</p>\n<p><strong>Async &amp; messaging:</strong> message queue, rate limiting, at-least-once delivery, idempotency key, saga pattern, two-phase commit, webhook, store-and-forward, fan-out-on-write, fan-out-on-read, hybrid fan-out.</p>\n<p><strong>Feed, search &amp; ranking:</strong> hot key/celebrity problem, top-K trending, sliding window aggregation, inverted index, PageRank/link analysis, candidate generation vs ranking, collaborative filtering, cold-start problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-time &amp; geo:</strong> WebRTC, SFU vs MCU, presence service, geohashing, quadtree, hot-zone problem, write batching, trip-state machine, three-sided marketplace.</p>\n<p><strong>Commerce &amp; payments:</strong> inventory reservation, order state machine, buy-box problem, settlement splitting, tokenization, PCI compliance, velocity checks, rules engine + ML scoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Storage &amp; sync:</strong> content-addressed storage, chunking/deduplication, delta sync, last-write-wins, metadata vs blob separation.</p>\n<p>Being able to produce a one-sentence definition for every term on this page, cold, without notes, is a fair bar for \"ready\" — that's the actual exit criterion for this week, more than any specific case study.</p>"}, {"num": 11, "title": "Timed Mock-Drill Practice", "subtitle": "10 unseen prompts · 45-minute drills · self-grading rubric", "tag": "Practice", "readMin": 4, "html": "<h1>Timed Mock-Drill Practice</h1>\n<p>Reading eight case studies is not the same skill as producing one live, under a clock, with someone watching you think. This week has no new material — it's ten unseen prompts, deliberately not covered in Weeks 2-9, for you to drill cold using the 7-step framework. The goal isn't to memorize answers to these ten; it's to notice, in a low-stakes setting, exactly where you freeze — and fix that before it happens in a real interview.</p>\n<h2>How to run a drill</h2>\n<p>Treat every prompt below as a real 45-minute round and actually set a timer. This pacing is the same shape a startup interviewer expects:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>0-5 min — Requirements.</strong> Ask yourself the clarifying questions from Week 1 out loud (or write them down). Don't skip this even when drilling alone — the habit has to be automatic, not something you remember to do only when someone's in the room.</li>\n<li><strong>5-10 min — APIs.</strong> Sketch 4-6 endpoints. Move on even if it doesn't feel complete.</li>\n<li><strong>10-15 min — Estimation.</strong> Pick round numbers, show the arithmetic, say the QPS/storage numbers out loud.</li>\n<li><strong>15-25 min — Data model + high-level design.</strong> Name your entities, pick SQL/NoSQL with a reason, draw the box-and-arrow flow.</li>\n<li><strong>25-40 min — Deep dive.</strong> Pick ONE component (never more than two) and go deep, the way Weeks 2-9 modeled it.</li>\n<li><strong>40-45 min — Bottlenecks and wrap.</strong> Name at least 3 scaling techniques by name, applied to this specific system, not generically.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Ten prompts to drill</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Design a URL shortener</strong> (like bit.ly). Forces you to practice pure estimation and key-space math with almost no distracting feature surface — a good first drill.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a rate limiter</strong> as a standalone service other systems call into. Tests whether you actually know the algorithms (token bucket, sliding window counter, leaky bucket) rather than just the word \"rate limiting.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Design a distributed key-value cache</strong> (a memcached/Redis-style system, not a product that uses one). Tests consistent hashing and cache-eviction policy knowledge directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a ticket-booking system</strong> (BookMyShow/Ticketmaster-style: concert or movie seats). This is deliberately close to Week 6's inventory-reservation pattern — a seat is exactly like a SKU with 1 unit of stock, and the \"hold a seat for 5 minutes during checkout\" behavior is the TTL-based reservation pattern named explicitly.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a notification system</strong> that fans a single event out to push, email, and SMS. Pulls directly from Week 3's fan-out and store-and-forward vocabulary — good test of Week 10's decomposition skill.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a web crawler</strong> as a standalone system (deeper than Week 9's usage of one) — politeness, frontier management, and dedup, on their own, without the ranking/serving half.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a parking garage / parking lot system.</strong> Lighter on distributed-systems concepts, heavier on clean data modeling and concurrency (two cars can't be assigned the same spot) — a good check that you don't over-engineer every prompt with Kafka and sharding when a well-modeled relational schema with a row-level lock is the right answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a collaborative whiteboard</strong> (or a simplified Google Docs). This is your Week 8 sibling-delta material (OT/CRDT) promoted to the main event — good test of whether you can go deep on a component you only saw as a delta, not a flagship.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a public API gateway</strong> with per-client rate limiting and authentication in front of several backend services. Tests whether you can reason about a system that sits <em>in front of</em> other systems rather than being the product itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Design a real-time leaderboard</strong> for a mobile game with millions of concurrent players. Pulls from Week 2's caching/fan-out instincts and forces a clean answer on \"how fresh does a rank need to be, really\" — a consistency-vs-latency judgment call, not a lookup you can memorize.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Self-grading rubric</h2>\n<p>After each drill, score yourself honestly against these — this is what an interviewer is actually checking for, more than whether your final architecture matches some canonical answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I ask at least 3 clarifying questions before drawing anything?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I state my assumptions out loud before doing any math?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I show the actual arithmetic for at least one estimate (QPS, storage, or bandwidth), not just a final number?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I explicitly justify my data-store choice (SQL vs NoSQL, or which NoSQL shape) rather than defaulting to Postgres out of habit?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I pick only 1-2 components to go deep on, and say out loud <em>why</em> those two?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I name at least 3 specific scaling techniques (not \"add more servers\") and explain why each applies <em>here</em>?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Did I finish within the time box, or did I run out of time still on requirements?</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you're consistently missing the same 1-2 boxes across several drills, that's your actual remaining gap — spend your last few practice sessions on that specific habit, not on covering more case studies.</p>"}, {"num": 12, "title": "Your Own Projects", "subtitle": "Where World Shiner and the rest actually fit", "tag": "Capstone", "readMin": 5, "html": "<h1>Capstone — Where Your Own Projects Actually Fit</h1>\n<p>Everything so far has been other companies' systems, at other companies' scale. This week is the bridge back to your own work — because the goal was never to memorize sixteen case studies, it was to get fluent enough in the underlying patterns that you can honestly, precisely describe what you've <em>actually built</em>, at the scale you <em>actually built it</em>. A startup interviewer doesn't expect you to have run anything at Twitter's scale. They're checking whether you can reason like someone who could grow into it.</p>\n<h2>Your projects, mapped to what you now know</h2>\n<p><strong>World Shiner 2.0</strong> (Node/Express/PostgreSQL, JWT across 5 role tiers, DB-level isolation across two company hubs, 85 tests, live in 7 countries) is your strongest case, and it now has real vocabulary attached to it. The 5-role JWT auth system is an <strong>authorization/RBAC design</strong> — say that phrase. The two-hub, DB-level isolation is a <strong>multi-tenancy pattern</strong>: you chose isolation-by-partition (each hub's data physically separated) over a single shared schema with a tenant_id column, the same trade-off Week 6's sellers-vs-single-merchant delta discusses — you can explain <em>why</em> you chose stronger isolation over the cheaper shared-schema approach (data-sensitivity and blast-radius reasons), which is exactly the \"state the trade-off, then justify your pick\" structure from Week 1, Step 6. The 85 tests catching a real authorization gap before production is a genuine correctness story — more valuable in an interview than a bigger, less-tested system.</p>\n<p><strong>Places Enrichment Console</strong> (FastAPI + React, third-party API integration, SSE progress, checkpointed processing) is your best \"distributed-systems-adjacent\" story after World Shiner. Checkpointing a long-running job so it can resume after a failure is the same idea as Week 5's async, decoupled processing pipelines — you built a smaller version of \"don't let a long job block the request path, and don't lose progress if it dies partway.\" Say it in those terms.</p>\n<p><strong>GS BULK PI and the Gemstone Bulk Upload Generator</strong> are ETL/batch-processing systems with a deterministic business-rules engine — precise, correct, well-tested transformation logic. This isn't system design at scale, and you shouldn't pretend it is; it's a legitimate <strong>data engineering</strong> story about correctness under messy real-world input (OCR noise, inconsistent formats), which is its own valued skill, distinct from what this course teaches.</p>\n<p><strong>Procurement Request Form</strong> is a straightforward, well-built workflow tool — fine to mention briefly, not worth over-selling as a systems-design example.</p>\n<p><strong>Customs Clearance Composer is your most interesting bridge, and worth understanding precisely.</strong> As shipped, it uses the MyMemory machine-translation API plus a learned, persisted dictionary (Firestore) — that is not an LLM and not RAG, and you should say so plainly if asked, rather than let an interviewer discover the gap themselves. But look at the shape of what you actually built: a <strong>retrieval step</strong> (look up the right phrase in your learned dictionary/glossary before falling back to raw translation) feeding <strong>generation</strong> (composing a structured, formatted email from the result). That two-step shape — retrieve relevant context, then generate — is <em>structurally</em> what a RAG pipeline is. You are one substitution away from a real, honest RAG demo: swap the MyMemory call for an LLM call, and pass your glossary's matched entries into that call's prompt as retrieved context, instead of only using the dictionary as a lookup table. That's not a hypothetical rewrite of the whole system — it's a scoped, believable weekend project if you want a genuine shipped RAG artifact to point to, rather than a claimed capability.</p>\n<p><strong>A&amp;A Creations' 9-tool platform</strong> is real, shipped, end-to-end SaaS tooling with rule-based (not ML) lead scoring — a legitimate \"I ship full products solo\" story, useful for culture-fit and ownership questions more than technical-depth questions.</p>\n<p><strong>AI Tools Directory</strong> is a content platform about AI tools, not an AI system itself — mention it only if asked about side projects, not as technical depth.</p>\n<h2>The honest scale caveat — and why it's not a weakness</h2>\n<p>None of your projects run at the DAU/QPS numbers in Weeks 2-9, and you don't need them to. The wrong move is either pretending otherwise or apologizing for it. The right line, if an interviewer probes: <em>\"I designed World Shiner for correctness, multi-tenant isolation, and maintainability at my actual scale — dozens of offices, not millions of users — but I can reason clearly about what changes at 100x that scale, because [name the specific technique from this course that would kick in first — e.g., 'the shared-schema-with-tenant-id trade-off from the e-commerce marketplace pattern' or 'sharding the tenant data by hub the way the payment ledger shards by account'].\"</em> That sentence does more work than a fabricated scale number would.</p>\n<h2>Final self-assessment, tied back to your prep timeline</h2>\n<p>Go back to the Technical Interview Prep Guide's Section 9 schedule and check these against where you actually are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Can I produce a one-sentence definition, cold, for every term in Week 10's vocabulary cheat-sheet?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Can I walk through World Shiner's architecture out loud, unprompted, using at least 4 terms from this course (RBAC, multi-tenancy, isolation, state machine, etc.)?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Have I run at least 2-3 timed drills from Week 11 and identified my own recurring gap from the rubric?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Can I honestly and confidently answer \"have you built a RAG system?\" without over-claiming — including the Customs Clearance Composer's real shape and what it would take to make it a real one?</span></li>\n<li class=\"tick-item\"><span class=\"tick-box\"></span><span>Do I know, for each of my real projects, which pattern family it's closest to and which it is <em>not</em>?</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If most of these are checked, you are precisely where CodeKredos said you could be: not bookish, not memorizing FAANG trivia, but able to hold a real technical conversation about real systems — which is exactly what a startup technical round at ₹12-15 LPA is built to find.</p>"}];
